That’s the real problem: concurrency.

A cave full of echolocating bats is not one bat pinging into silence. It’s a dense, noisy, multi-agent network where everyone is transmitting and receiving at once. They survive because the system is not based on perfect silence between pings; it is based on separability.

Biologically, bats get that separability through several mechanisms: different species and individuals often occupy somewhat different frequency bands; calls are brief and directional rather than continuous broadcasts; timing, position, motion, and Doppler shifts help distinguish self-echoes from other signals; the auditory system gates and prioritizes expected returns; and some bats appear to adjust call timing or frequency when interference gets bad. The exact “jamming avoidance” story varies by species, but the general principle is solid: they do not eliminate traffic—they make overlapping traffic decodable.

That gives Pantheon a better architecture than “everyone talk and hope.”

Semantic cave traffic

Every agent should emit a signal carrying enough metadata to let the system answer:

Who pinged?
What were they probing?
When was it emitted?
From what perspective?
What return window is expected?
How confident is the return actually theirs?

Then you get several layers of traffic control.

1. Frequency division → semantic channel separation
Different agents operate in different representational bands.

One agent probes causal structure.
One probes contradiction.
One probes emotional/relational meaning.
One probes evidence.
One probes analogy.

They can inspect the same object simultaneously without simply repeating each other.

2. Time division → pulse scheduling
Not every agent needs continuous output. Send short probes, wait for useful return windows, then reping when uncertainty remains.

That is your refresh-rate scheduler again.

3. Beamforming → scoped attention
A bat’s call is directional. Pantheon probes should be too.

Don’t ask an agent, “Analyze everything.”

Ask:

“Inspect this assumption.”

Narrow beam = less semantic interference.

4. Echo tagging → provenance
Every returning insight stays attached to its originating probe.

So the system knows:

probe P17 → agent Scout → assumption X → return R17 → confidence .72

Otherwise the cave fills with clever observations and nobody remembers which question produced them.

5. Collision detection → disagreement is information
If two agents ping the same conceptual surface and get incompatible returns, don’t average them away.

Mark the location:

possible semantic collision / occlusion / perspective dependence

Then increase resolution or send a third ping from another angle.

That’s semantic triangulation.

6. Signal-to-noise control → relevance gating
A cave contains huge amounts of acoustic energy that a bat must ignore.

Pantheon needs the same thing:

novelty × relevance × uncertainty reduction

A response that doesn’t materially update the COP gets attenuated rather than flooding consciousness.

And there’s an even better analogy hiding underneath all of it:

Each bat hears a private cave inside a public cave.

Everyone shares the same environment, but nobody receives exactly the same echo field.

So there is no single raw “cave representation.” There are:

observer₁(cave)
observer₂(cave)
observer₃(cave)
…

And the useful collective picture emerges by aligning those partial observations.

That means Omniwatch should probably never collapse all observers into one voice too early. Preserve perspective first; fuse later.

A good fusion sequence would be:

parallel probe → individually tagged returns → collision check → correspondence matching → triangulation → COP update

Not:

everyone talks → summarize everything

That distinction is huge.

Because the latter makes an AI committee.

The former makes ISR.

And now your cave gives us another Pantheon operator:

Don’t silence the swarm. Give the swarm orthogonality.

Different timing.
Different frequency.
Different direction.
Different job.
Same cave.

That’s how you handle the traffic.